Open1602 Vita source distribution

Open1602 and the Vita adaptations: GPL-3.0-or-later; see LICENSE and file headers.
Original project: https://github.com/x4rd4s/open1602
Original base: 3fde8d11ca85cc783428c96839f3fd80d4827adb (Open1602 0.3.1).

Modified distribution prepared by Colie286 on 2026-11-10. It includes the native
Vita platform, rendering, audio, controls, networking, settings and cache changes
from the Vita port, and a standalone Vita build. Compared with the local 1.5
release, the default master endpoint is empty; a user may supply master_server
in settings.cfg. LAN, Direct-IP and Ad-hoc remain available.

This source export contains no commercial game resources. The DE/EN catalogues
and balance preset are Open1602 project sources. The two embedded window icons
are original Open1602 CC0 artwork; see data/icons/window/LICENSE.txt.
The three Open1602 LiveArea PNGs were supplied by the project owner as
AI-generated artwork with permission to redistribute them under GPL-3.0-or-later.

Bundled libraries retain their original notices: ENet 1.3.18 (MIT), Lua 5.4.8
(MIT), stb_vorbis (MIT/public-domain option), dr_flac (MIT-0/public-domain option).
The video/audio decoder files retain their upstream LGPL and other notices.
SDL3 and zlib are pinned public submodules; the Vita SDL patches are included.
Full linked-component notices are in platform/vita/licenses/ThirdParty.txt,
SDL3.txt and zlib.txt. Corresponding source revisions for SDK components are in
platform/vita/licenses/SOURCES.txt. Preserve these notices when redistributing.

To build, initialize the submodules, set VITASDK, then run
bash tools/vita/build-sdl3.sh and bash tools/vita/build.sh.
Requires Git, CMake >= 3.25, Python 3 with Pillow, a build tool and VitaSDK with
C++20 support and the Vita SDL3 backend dependencies. Build output is
build/vita/platform/vita/open1602.vpk. No game installation is needed to compile.

Binary distribution requires the complete corresponding source and applicable
LGPL relinking materials for the exact release, including dependencies and
build scripts. Providing notices alone does not fulfill these obligations.
